  {
    "episode_id": 42503,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42503.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "Hey, hey, good afternoon and welcome. This is what doesn't kill you. Food industry insights. I'm your host, Katie Kiefer, and we have a really special show today. I was up in Rhode Island where I'm often want to be last week, and I arranged to have an interview with my senator, Sheldon Whitehouse, the Democrat from Rhode Island. And Senator Whitehouse has been basically a gadfly in the side of the Senate of Congress in its entirety because of the 25-minute speeches that he gives every single week that Congress is in session and has done for the last almost three years. So I think he's up to about 127, 128 of these speeches, each one dealing with a very specific topic related to climate change. So he's a bit of a rock star hero in my mind. I'm not quite on the level of David Bowie alas, but nevertheless still quite a remarkable guy. And this is an interesting conversation. Like I initially thought we would talk about sort of food policy and do we need a national food policy director and so forth. And then that's not his bailiwick. It's not his wheelhouse. His thing is is oceans and fisheries because he's the Rhode Island state senator and that's a big industry for Rhode Island. So he's very interesting on that subject but he's very, the really interesting part about this interview I thought was sort of explaining or laying bare the mechanisms of lobbying and how it works. So we had a lot of talk about sort of Companies like Unilever and General Foods, Nestle, and so forth, Pepsi and Coke, and their attitudes toward climate change. He has some interesting opinions on that. And then listen for the part where I ask him about whether or not the fossil fuel energy could ever be sued for Rico, which is racketeering the way the tobacco industry was. And he has a couple of really sort of almost terrifying comments about that. Anyway, stay tuned and we're going to have a quick sponsor drop before we go into the tape and then you will be hearing me with Senator Sheldon Whitehouse from the State of Rhode Island talking about fossil fuel lobbying and the impact of climate change on agriculture going forward. I",
