  {
    "episode_id": 42503,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42503.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "There were some very dark days for the climate movement after the Waxman-Markeyville passed the house. For some reason, the administration completely lost interest in climate change as an issue. We wouldn't pass anything on the Senate that would have gotten at least into a conference with the House bill that people had heroically got their careers to pass",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "There were some very dark days for the climate movement after the Waxman-Markey bill passed the House.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "After the Waxman-Markey bill passed the House, the administration completely lost interest in climate change as an issue.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The Senate did not pass any legislation that would have entered into a conference with the House version of the Waxman-Markey bill.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "People involved in passing the House bill risked or sacrificed their careers to advance it.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The passage of the House bill was a significant milestone for the climate movement.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The lack of Senate action following the House passage indicates a failure by leadership or political will.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The administration’s loss of interest in climate policy undermines progress toward meaningful environmental reform.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Career risks taken by activists reflect high moral stakes and personal investment in climate justice.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "A conference between House and Senate versions is necessary for legislative success.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 42503,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42503.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "And after that, there was this long period in which the Obama administration would not use the words climate and change in the same paragraph. and there was silence on the Senate floor, and yet the facts and the evidence kept piling up. So I just wanted to make sure that people knew that somebody at least was still paying attention to reassure environmentalists that there was, you know, the fire had not completely gone out and to prod our Republican colleagues to pay attention to this. And frankly, at the time, all sort of prod the administration, which was not having its finest hour on this issue.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Factual evidence related to climate change continued to accumulate over time.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "The Obama administration avoided using the terms 'climate' and 'change' together in the same paragraph.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
